name  = binarytrees
shape = allocate a great many small nodes, walk them, discard them
args  = 16
claim = the shape the region model is built around, and the one where its two
        halves pull in opposite directions. Allocation is a bump -- no header to
        fill in, no free list to consult, no collector to trace -- so the TIME
        should be good; and nothing is ever handed back, so every discarded tree
        stays resident and the FOOTPRINT should be bad. A row that splits is
        worth more than one that wins.
        The C row frees each tree after checking it, which is what a C
        programmer writes and is why its footprint stays flat. A C version that
        bump-allocated into an arena and never freed would look like the Mere
        row -- that is the comparison Mere is making, not a different algorithm.
        bench_region.mere puts each depth's batch inside a `region R { }` block,
        which is the language's answer, and pays a copy of the answer out.

# Total allocation across every arena, at v0.1.321. The two Mere rows sit at
# almost the same point (353,371,856 B naive; 3,147,344 + 350,224,512 B for the
# region version) and that is the finding: a `region R { }` whose result is an
# int copies almost nothing out, so bounding the residency is FREE here. What
# it changes is peak -- 337 MiB against 63 MiB, and 190 ms against 87 ms,
# because the naive version's working set stops fitting anywhere near the CPU.
#
# Contrast churn, where the same idea costs 44% more cumulative allocation: a
# loop has to deep-copy its live set into each new generation, and a block only
# has to copy its answer.
alloc_min = 268435456
alloc_max = 419430400

# The number the region block exists to control.
peak_max = 100663296

# MoonBit needs strconv for the depth argument.
mbt_imports = moonbitlang/core/env moonbitlang/core/strconv
